>>how do I pass the embedding layer into the model so that its parameters are included in the model?”, it works like this:
```py
import tensorflow as tf
from tensorflow.keras import layers
# 1. Define your vocabulary
vocab_size = 100_000
# 2. Define embedding size
embedding_dim = 4096
# 3. Create the input
input_ids = tf.keras.Input(
    shape=(None,),
    dtype=tf.int32,
    name="input_ids"
)
# 4. Create the embedding layer
embeddings = layers.Embedding(
    input_dim=vocab_size,
    output_dim=embedding_dim,
    name="token_embedding"
)(input_ids)
# 5. Continue your model
x = embeddings
# Example transformer/model layers would go here
x = layers.Dense(4096, activation="gelu")(x)
# 6. Create the model
model = tf.keras.Model(
    inputs=input_ids,
    outputs=x,
    name="LMLM"
)
# 7. Build it
model(tf.zeros((1, 10), dtype=tf.int32))
# 8. See parameter count
model.summary()

The important part is this:

layers.Embedding(
    input_dim=vocab_size,
    output_dim=embedding_dim
)
```

# That creates the actual embedding weights:
```tts
100,000 × 4,096
= 409,600,000 parameters

Those 409.6M parameters automatically become part of model.count_params().
```
* So you don’t do:
```
model.parameters = 5_104_298_467  # ❌

Instead:

embedding → transformer layers → output
                    ↓
                  model
                    ↓
             count_params()

```
building  LMLM

If your goal is to have something like:

LMLM-5.1B
├── Tokenizer
├── Embedding
├── Transformer blocks
├── Normalization
├── Output head
└── 5,104,298,467 total parameters

then the embedding must be instantiated inside the model before you call model.summary().

If you show me your current LMLM model code, especially the part where you create the tokenizer/embedding and transformer, I can show you exactly where to insert it and how to make the 5.104B count match.
